      "message": "Some Gemini review feedback below. I don\u0027t think the recursive mutex is too bad, so feel free to keep it as is. If you want to change things I would do it in a followup CL.\n\n----\nHere is the refactored code demonstrating the unlocked architectural approach.\n\nBy separating the core logic from the locking mechanism, we avoid recursive mutex acquisitions, improve overall performance, and make the underlying logic easier to unit test. I have also included the explicit error checking for the protection flags discussed in the review.\n\n### 1. Extract the Unlocked Helpers\n\nFirst, we extract the core logic of `get_protect_flags` (and similarly for `protect_at_boot`) into internal `_unlocked` static functions.\n\n```c\n/* Internal helper: Get protection flags without acquiring the lock */\nstatic uint32_t _cros_flash_andes_xip_get_protect_flags_unlocked(const struct device *dev)\n{\n\tuint32_t flags \u003d 0;\n\tstruct andes_xip_ex_ops_get_out op_out;\n\tunsigned int prot_start;\n\tunsigned int prot_end;\n\tunsigned int prot_len;\n\tint ret;\n\tbool status_locked;\n\n\tret \u003d flash_andes_xip_get_lock_status(dev, \u0026status_locked);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\treturn EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_UNKNOWN;\n\t}\n\n\tret \u003d flash_andes_xip_get_status_regs(dev, \u0026op_out);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\treturn EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_UNKNOWN;\n\t}\n\n\t/* Check for inconsistent protection states */\n\tif ((op_out.regs[0] \u0026 SPI_FLASH_SR1_SRP0) ||\n\t    (op_out.regs[1] \u0026 SPI_FLASH_SR2_SRP1) ||\n\t    (op_out.regs[2] \u0026 SPI_FLASH_CR_WPS)) {\n\t\treturn EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_INCONSISTENT;\n\t}\n\n\tret \u003d spi_flash_reg_to_protect(op_out.regs[0], op_out.regs[1],\n\t\t\t\t       \u0026prot_start, \u0026prot_len);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\treturn EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_UNKNOWN;\n\t}\n\n\tif (prot_start !\u003d FLASH_PROTECTION_START) {\n\t\treturn EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_INCONSISTENT;\n\t}\n\tprot_end \u003d prot_start + prot_len;\n\n\t/* Overlap checks */\n\tif (prot_end \u003e\u003d (CONFIG_RW_MEM_OFF + CONFIG_RW_SIZE)) {\n\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ALL_AT_BOOT |\n\t\t\t EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_AT_BOOT;\n#ifdef CONFIG_ROLLBACK\n\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ROLLBACK_AT_BOOT;\n\t} else if (prot_end \u003e\u003d (CONFIG_ROLLBACK_OFF + CONFIG_ROLLBACK_SIZE)) {\n\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_AT_BOOT |\n\t\t\t EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ROLLBACK_AT_BOOT;\n#endif /* CONFIG_ROLLBACK */\n\t} else if (prot_end \u003e\u003d\n\t\t   (CONFIG_WP_STORAGE_OFF + CONFIG_WP_STORAGE_SIZE)) {\n\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_AT_BOOT;\n\t}\n\n\t/* Status locked flag additions */\n\tif (status_locked) {\n\t\tif (flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ALL_AT_BOOT) {\n\t\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ALL_NOW |\n\t\t\t\t EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_NOW;\n#ifdef CONFIG_ROLLBACK\n\t\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ROLLBACK_NOW;\n\t\t} else if (flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ROLLBACK_AT_BOOT) {\n\t\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ROLLBACK_NOW |\n\t\t\t\t EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_NOW;\n#endif /* CONFIG_ROLLBACK */\n\t\t} else if (flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_AT_BOOT) {\n\t\t\tflags |\u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_NOW;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn flags;\n}\n\n/* Public API: Acquires the lock, then calls the helper */\nstatic uint32_t cros_flash_andes_xip_get_protect_flags(const struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct cros_flash_andestech_xip_data *data \u003d DRV_DATA(dev);\n\tuint32_t flags;\n\n\tk_mutex_lock(\u0026data-\u003eflash_lock, K_FOREVER);\n\tflags \u003d _cros_flash_andes_xip_get_protect_flags_unlocked(dev);\n\tk_mutex_unlock(\u0026data-\u003eflash_lock);\n\n\treturn flags;\n}\n\n```\n\n*(Note: You would apply this exact same `_unlocked` pattern to `cros_flash_andes_xip_protect_at_boot` before utilizing it in the init function).*\n\n### 2. Rewrite the Init Function\n\nNow we can rewrite `cros_flash_andes_xip_init` to safely utilize the unlocked helpers, maintaining a single locking context throughout the function\u0027s execution. It also implements the explicit error-checking step.\n\n```c\nstatic int cros_flash_andes_xip_init(const struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct cros_flash_andestech_xip_data *data \u003d DRV_DATA(dev);\n\tstruct andes_xip_ex_ops_set_in op_in \u003d { .regs \u003d { 0 } };\n\tint ret;\n\n\t/* Make sure to clear SRP bits - only software protection. */\n\top_in.masks[0] \u003d SPI_FLASH_SR1_SRP0;\n\top_in.masks[1] \u003d SPI_FLASH_SR2_SRP1;\n\t/* Make sure to clear WPS bit - per block protection. */\n\top_in.masks[2] \u003d SPI_FLASH_CR_WPS;\n\n\tk_mutex_lock(\u0026data-\u003eflash_lock, K_FOREVER);\n\tret \u003d flash_andes_xip_set_status_regs(dev, \u0026op_in);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\tgoto unlock_flash;\n\t}\n\n\tif (!system_is_in_rw()) {\n\t\t/* Call the unlocked helper since we already hold flash_lock */\n\t\tuint32_t flags \u003d _cros_flash_andes_xip_get_protect_flags_unlocked(dev);\n\t\t\n\t\t/* Explicitly verify no errors were returned before processing flags */\n\t\tif (!(flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_UNKNOWN) \u0026\u0026 \n\t\t    !(flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_INCONSISTENT)) {\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tif (flags \u0026 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ALL_AT_BOOT) {\n\t\t\t\t/* Call the unlocked helper for applying protection */\n\t\t\t\tret \u003d _cros_flash_andes_xip_protect_at_boot_unlocked(\n\t\t\t\t\tdev, EC_FLASH_PROTECT_RO_AT_BOOT);\n\t\t\t\tif (ret) {\n\t\t\t\t\tgoto unlock_flash;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tif (write_protect_is_asserted()) {\n\t\t/* Emulate behaviour of #WP pin and the lock status registers. */\n\t\tret \u003d flash_andes_xip_lock_status(dev, true);\n\t}\n\nunlock_flash:\n\tk_mutex_unlock(\u0026data-\u003eflash_lock);\n\treturn ret;\n}\n\n```",
